  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =6\\sqrt{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =6+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=6\\sqrt{t+1}-\\frac{11}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =6\\sqrt{3+1}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =6(2)-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{13}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
